WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.200
Human to animal shapeshifting creatures are
a worldwide phenomenon.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.500
Were-predatory beasts exist in a myriad of
forms:&nbsp;

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:15.079
werehyenas, weretigers, werepanthers, werejaguars…
but the werewolf is the most notorious of

00:00:15.079 --> 00:00:16.079
them all.&nbsp;

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:20.050
There’s so much history behind the infamous
shapeshifters that we’re going to take two

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.750
episodes to trace the beginnings of the werewolf
in Europe through their entrance into modern

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.320
literature and pop culture.&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.330
The first thing to understand about the history
of the werewolf is that there is no single

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.340
origin.&nbsp;

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.660
Legends of these lupine shapeshifters are
so numerous and widespread, their pervasiveness

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.199
makes identifying one universal definition
or origin of the wolf-human monster challenging.&nbsp;

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:46.340
Despite the werewolf ’s widespread appearance
in folklore, I’ll admit I haven’t really

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.339
given the werewolf the attention it deserves—until
now.

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:57.629
[Monstrum intro]
​​The wolf has symbolized everything from

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:03.530
an admirable patron of warriors, to figures
for greed, and even as the Devil in disguise.&nbsp;

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.000
These near-silent, deadly predators were at
one point the most widely distributed land

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.270
mammal, claiming large swaths of the globe
as their hunting grounds—and wolves posed

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.100
a very real threat to humans and their livestock.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.549
Still, we seemed to have admired the animals.

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:24.870
Our ancestors donned the skins of predators
while hunting, perhaps to camouflage themselves,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.759
or to evoke the spirit of an animal during
the hunt, as archeological artifacts and ancient

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:30.829
art reveal.&nbsp;

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:35.020
Humans and wolves existed as both allies and
enemies.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.770
While some wolves were eventually domesticated
for hunting and protection—

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.570
their wild brethren remained a threat.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.049
These early connections set the stage for
a complex relationship between man, wolf,

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:47.049
and were-hybrid.

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:52.369
But does that mean werewolves were originally
a universal monster, or were there other factors

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:53.759
that came into play?

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:58.539
To understand the origins of the werewolves
more clearly, I reached out to Dr. Kaja Franck,

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:03.149
werewolf expert and visiting professor at
the University of Hertfordshire]

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:07.079
Wolves themselves exists in many different
countries and in many different continents.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:12.650
And because of this, they have become powerful
symbols of the animal, other.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.920
Wolves, and their behavior is often very similar
to how humans exist.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.769
They live in families and they hunt as packs
and they predate on the same creatures that

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:26.069
we'd like to eat ourselves.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:30.860
And so it makes sense that folklore would
absorb these creatures and they would recur

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.989
in myths and in legends.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.180
And he would, would see them as existing on
the boundaries of what it was to be human

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.450
and what it was to be animal.&nbsp;

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.239
The connections between wolves and humans
are deeply ingrained in ancient literature

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:45.470
and lore.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:50.980
These early stories seem to categorize werewolves
into three different types: the “victim”

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:56.180
where an innocent person is cursed to turn
into a wolf but maintains human reason; the

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.660
“damned” who are violent humans that become
wolves as a punishment to both their body

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.349
and soul; and the “warrior” where humans
take on the guise of the wolf to aid in success

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:06.560
in battle.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.720
The oldest piece of surviving literature we
know about —The Epic of Gilgamesh—portrays

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.530
a “victim” werewolf in what is widely
considered the oldest werewolf story on record.&nbsp;

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:22.019
Written between 1300 and 1000 BCE it tells
of an unlucky shepherd whom the goddess Inanna,

00:03:22.019 --> 00:03:23.769
turns into a wolf.&nbsp;

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:28.069
In contrast, werewolves of Ancient Greece
and Roman mythology are dominantly portrayed

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:32.879
as the “damned” werewolf, representing
a tradition of human to wolf transformations

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.269
distinguished by deviant deeds.

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:40.060
In the Greek text, Agriopas’ Olympionics,
a man is turned into a wolf for 10 years as

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.310
punishment for eating human entrails.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.950
Cannibalism is a recurring theme in ancient
myths that use wolf transformation as a cautionary

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:48.950
tale.&nbsp;

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:54.560
Linking human cannibalism to wolves isn’t
that far-fetched; wolves are above all opportunistic

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.770
eaters.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:59.980
In addition to scavenging animal corpses of
all kinds—including humans—wolves have

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.049
been known to eat the remains of their own
species.&nbsp;

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:09.590
In the first century CE, Pliny wrote Naturalis
Historia, associating human cannibalism with

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.790
wolf-like behavior and features.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:17.190
About 8 CE, Ovid’s Metamorphoses interprets
another Greek legend about the god Zeus and

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.180
King Lycaon of Arcadia.&nbsp;

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.590
According to the story, the Roman god Jupiter
hears rumors that King Lycaon is practicing

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:29.449
human sacrifice and cannibalism, so he descends
to earth in human form to discover the truth.

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:34.270
Lycaon is unconvinced of Jupiter’s divinity,
and decides to put his omniscience to the

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.270
test.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.380
He kills, butchers, and attempts to serve
human flesh to the god.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:44.340
Jupiter is appalled by the crime, and the
deception, so he destroys the king’s household

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.880
in a mad rage.&nbsp;

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.410
A distraught Lycaon takes to the fields—howling.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:54.090
Driven mad by his own misdeeds, he begins
to slaughter the flocks as a horrible transformation

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.220
takes place.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:00.470
His clothes become hair, his arms turn to
legs—and he transforms fully into a wolf,

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.960
although his human eyes remain.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.430
If the name Lycaon sounds familiar, your wolf
sense might be tingling.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.060
The Greek word ‘lykos’ meaning ‘wolf’
and ‘anthropos’ meaning ‘man,’ inspired

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.169
the word lycanthropy or “wolf-man syndrome.”&nbsp;

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:20.350
The word was invented in the early second
century by the Greek physician Marcellus to

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.690
define a melancholy affliction&nbsp;

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:27.470
identified by the habit of frequenting cemeteries
and behaving like wolves and dogs.&nbsp;

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:34.110
The third ancient werewolf trope, the “warrior,”
is perhaps most famously associated with berserkers,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.250
Ancient Nordic fighters who took on a predatory
animal’s characteristics in battle.&nbsp;

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:44.260
Bears, wolves, and boars were revered by berserkers,
and it was believed that literally donning

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.509
the skin of an animal allowed a transformation
to occur.&nbsp;

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:54.039
They would fight with crazed, seemingly preternatural
violence, howling and roaring on the battlefield.

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:59.069
Instead of separating humans from beasts,
this concept respects both in tandem.

00:05:59.069 --> 00:06:04.870
However, psychological and physical shapeshifting
were seen as different concepts.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:09.770
The berserker took on the spirit of the animal
but did not physically change form.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:15.180
In the 13th century collection, Saga of the
Volsungs , werewolves appear multiple times.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:20.520
In one story, a princess named Signy marries
a rival king, who in war kills her father

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.090
and captures her 10 brothers.&nbsp;

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.090
&nbsp;

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.229
One by one, the brothers are eaten by a large
mysterious she-wolf.

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:31.050
This happens every night until only one, Sigmund,
is left.&nbsp;

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:36.080
With his sister's help and a little honey,,
Sigmund kills the wolf, believed to be a human

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.169
woman in disguise, by biting off her tongue."

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:44.669
The now-dead creature is believed to be Signy's
mother-in-law who had taken on a wolf form

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:45.680
through witchcraft.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.720
In another example from the Saga, a thieving
father and son duo find two magic wolf pelts

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:52.850
in a forest hut.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:58.310
They put on the skins, transform into wolves,
and are horrified to learn they cannot transform

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:03.600
back; unbeknownst to them, the skins can only
be removed every 10 days.&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.389
In wolf form, they decide to separate but
if they meet too much trouble on their own,

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:09.510
they should howl for help.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:15.729
The son ignores this warning and manages to
kill 11 men before severely injuring himself.

00:07:15.729 --> 00:07:18.690
After the 10 days pass, both men return to
human form.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:22.560
Horrified by the experience they burn the
magic pelts.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.479
As thieves, the two live separated from society,
embodying wolfish tendencies before turning

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:29.460
into literal wolves.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.610
In the Norse language the word for wolf is
varg.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:37.650
Since wolves were considered withdrawn and
solitary, varg inspired the word vargr—which

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:39.990
roughly translates to outlaw.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.020
While some etymologists argue that the ‘wer’
in “werewolf’ comes from the Anglo-Saxon

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:48.390
word for ‘man’ other’s claim that the
extra ‘e,’ and the common variations of

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:51.490
‘war-‘ and ‘varg’ challenge this idea.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:56.270
One thing that does seem clear is that from
the beginning of their infamous name, werewolves

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:00.610
have been associated with solitude and violence.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.680
The etymology itself is somewhat complicated
and not always clear.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.750
It said that it could come from the old English
or from the old German or from a mixture of

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:10.750
two.&nbsp;

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:16.090
What does seem to be clear is that it specifically
refers to the word man Wolf and by man, I

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:21.271
don't mean how we use the term man today,
meaning mankind, but rather masculine or male,

00:08:21.271 --> 00:08:24.150
the male or masculine human.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:28.520
And so werewolf necessarily means a male monster.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:32.260
And this certainly has been the case that
we do find that there are definitely more

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.600
male werewolves than there are female werewolves.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.130
The Germanic Nordic traditions spread across
Europe as its people did and that’s why

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.920
werewolf stories are found in indigenous Icelandic
sources

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.730
It’s also likely that Celtic werewolf stories
were likewise influenced by the Ancient Nordic

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:53.881
people, especially given that the strongest
werewolf tradition comes from Ossory, an area

00:08:53.881 --> 00:08:56.380
with strong historical ties to vikings.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.630
As the werewolf legend spread, different cultures
and geographic areas contributed their own

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:06.390
unique flourishes—resulting in a staggering
number of werewolf variations.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:08.390
&nbsp;
Serbian legend says one becomes a werewolf

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:11.210
if you drink water from the footprint of a
wolf.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:15.170
Danish tradition dictates that only Danish
men can become werewolves.&nbsp;

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:19.410
Russian folk belief claims that a witch placing
her girdle under a house’s threshold on

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.590
the inhabitant’s wedding day will turn all
who are celebrating into wolves once they

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:25.020
cross over it.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:29.430
The introduction of Christianity saw these
ancient beliefs and myths rewritten as “pagan”

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:34.111
and therefore sinful—so the werewolf became
something to be disproved and denied, through

00:09:34.111 --> 00:09:37.260
mental illness or demonic influence.&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:42.450
Human to wolf transformation would be a defiance
of divinity—only God had the power to alter

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.100
something so completely.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.940
The first recorded use of the word “werewolf”
appears in the 11th century in the Ecclesiastical

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:53.010
Ordinances of King Cnut (c. 1016-1035), where
it’s used symbolically to warn Christian

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:56.340
clergy to be on the lookout for those who
might threaten their congregation.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:01.540
The differences and similarities in European
werewolves under Christianity reflect the

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:05.720
dominant anxieties and values of Europe during
the Middle Ages.&nbsp;

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:10.450
One such example emerged in the recurring
trope of the “Sympathetic Werewolf,” where

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:14.600
a human soul trapped in a wolf body could
be divinely saved if they showed their devotion

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:15.600
to God.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.910
Celtic traditions of werewolves from the time
developed to shape the creature into victims

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.800
suffering from involuntary transformation,
a sympathetic interpretation that reflects

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.780
the heavy influence of Christianity in the
country,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.950
as illustrated in archdeacon and historian
Gerald of Wales’s account of the landscape

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.290
and people of Ireland.&nbsp;

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:37.710
Published in the late 12th Century, Topographia
Hibernica, narrates an encounter between a

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:42.550
priest and a werewolf where the monster asks
the holy man for communion and last rites

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:47.260
for his wife—who is also a werewolf—a
fact that is revealed when the desperate male

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:51.520
werewolf pulls back her wolf skin to reveal
an elderly woman.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.110
Perhaps the singular most important ‘Sympathetic
Werewolf’ tale of the 12th century is located

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.930
in Marie de France’s Bisclavret.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:02.930
Published in the latter part of the century,
the French poem of a werewolf knight was translated

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:06.330
into Old Norse—broadening its readership.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:11.390
The story focuses on a handsome, happily married
noble knight—who just so happens to disappear

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:13.390
from home every three days.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:17.460
His wife accuses him of having an affair,
and he won’t defend his absence saying the

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.470
truth will only harm her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:25.230
The wife asks him again and again for an explanation
until finally, he says “My lady, I turn

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:30.490
bisclavret;/ I plunge into that great forest./
In thick woods I like it best./ I live on

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:32.080
what prey I can get.”&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:36.690
He’s naked when this happens and will not
tell her where he hides his clothes because

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.730
if they are lost he could stay a werewolf
forever.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:43.680
After a bit of nagging, he reveals they are
under a bush at an old chapel.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.280
The terrified wife devises a plan to escape
the marriage.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.580
She and her lover steal her husband’s clothes
and when he never returns home, the lovers

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:52.730
marry.&nbsp;

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:57.200
A year later, the missing husband’s friend,
the King, is hunting in their woods when he

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:59.550
comes across Bisclavret in werewolf form.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:06.050
Instead of killing the beast, the King spares
his life after the wolf shows his human side—kissing

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:07.200
his foot and leg.&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.420
The werewolf joins the King’s retinue and
is well cared for.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:15.760
All is well until Bisclavret's wife’s new
husband attends the King’s feast and the

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.940
werewolf bites the knight and drags him away,
although the king manages to stop him from

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:21.940
killing the man.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:26.030
Bisclavret is violent again when the King’s
hunting party comes across his scheming wife

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:29.100
and he attacks the woman—tearing off her
nose and face.&nbsp;

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:34.480
He is about to be killed when a “wise fellow”
proposes that his violence against these two

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:40.510
people shows he has a great grudge and speculates
the beast is the missing knight.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:44.520
Under the King’s questioning, in pain and
fear the lady confesses to stealing the clothing.&nbsp;

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:49.710
The King demands its return, but Bisclavret
ignores the clothes, too ashamed to show the

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.710
“terrible” change “from the form of
a beast.”

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:56.710
Only when he is alone does Bisclavret don
the clothes, turning back to human form.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:00.270
The lady and her knight are expelled from
the realm,&nbsp;

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:04.290
and in an interesting extension of the “werewolf’s
curse,” some of their female progeny are

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.360
born without noses.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.940
Other European werewolf traditions grew as
well.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.050
The “Sympathetic Werewolf” was joined
by the “Werewolf Lover.”

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:16.330
In these tales, a woman is attacked by a wolf
who rips off some of her clothes, usually

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:17.720
her apron or skirt.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:22.760
Later, when he returns in human form, the
woman recognizes him as the werewolf by the

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:24.580
clothing or threads between his teeth.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:25.580
In some versions, the women escape by throwing
off their clothing, which the werewolf attacks

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:26.580
instead.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.730
Scholars often read these stories as symbolic
of sexual assault against women.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:33.520
There is also the “Wounded Werewolf” a
trope where the male human form of the werewolf

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:37.790
is only revealed when the beast is struck
by a knife or a piece of wood.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:41.530
Now that his human identity is revealed, he
is no longer a threat.&nbsp;

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:46.370
Then there are the “Back Rider” or “Wolf-Rider”
stories, where a man transforms into a werewolf

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.990
with the help of a special belt or skin.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:52.290
These werewolves would cling to the back of
a male victim.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:57.680
During the Middle Ages, werewolf legends evolved
and expanded, becoming more widespread—and

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:59.450
more demonized by the Church.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.350
Reports of werewolf activity increased.&nbsp;

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:07.610
Barry Lopez and his book of wolves and men
suggest that one of the reasons why the way

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:12.410
that the werewolf was changing in terms of
depiction was in part because of the way wolves

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:18.890
were being seen in the 14 hundreds in Europe,
farming was changing and it was becoming more,

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:23.690
um, about sheep and sheep became a center
of the economy within Europe.

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:28.420
Now, unfortunately not only did humans like
sheep, but so did wolves.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:32.940
And suddenly there was a sense that wolves
were predating, that wolves were destroying

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:40.870
expensive sheep&nbsp;

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:46.510
and because of the preexisting symbolism within,
uh, religion, within Christianity, regarding

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:54.350
the Wolf and sheep, as God's flock, it quite
nicely fitted in together that the Wolf became

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.350
seen as more monstrous and monstrous.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:59.740
And therefore the wealth became seen as more
and more monstrous.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:04.980
And that a transformation into the Wolf was
necessarily an evil thing, most likely caused

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.830
by the enemy of God, the devil.&nbsp;

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:13.230
As werewolf stories continued to rise in popularity,
the Church’s stance on the creature changed.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:16.910
Where before, it was an official sin in the
Christian Church to believe in werewolves

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:20.630
because it suggested that beings other than
God could alter physical substance.&nbsp;

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:25.200
However, beginning in the later 16th century
it was considered heretical to not believe

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.590
in werewolves, a change that came after the
Church shifted its attention to persecuting

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:30.600
witches.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:36.960
This change seems to be an attempt again,
to deal with the proliferation of werewolves,

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:41.950
but also to deal with the fact that the belief
and the myth of Wells doesn't seem to be going

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:42.950
anywhere.

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:47.750
And so it made more sense to suggest that
the devil literally could transform man into

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:48.750
Wolf.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:52.470
And I think this is because the church saw
that it was far better to keep responding

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:57.860
and adapting and absorbing these beliefs rather
than absolutely refuting them.&nbsp;

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:02.630
Shape-changing into wolves became a component
in witchcraft accusations and was often accompanied

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:04.980
by charges of cannibalism.&nbsp;

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:09.700
Part of the rise of the so-called “werewolf
trials” is also due to a seminal text on

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:13.320
identifying and punishing witches— the Malleus
Maleficarum.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:18.940
Co-authored by Catholic clergyman and inquisitors
Heinrich Kraemer and Johann Sprenger, after

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:25.810
its publication in 1486, this legal and theological
text not only became the standard for witch-hunting,

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:29.620
it served as a handbook for the approved torture
of those accused.&nbsp;

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:33.960
The text claimed some witches were able to
turn into wolves, although this metamorphosis

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:37.630
was an illusion rather than a true transformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:41.790
It was the “Devil” who helped produce
glamours, a kind of magic that makes things

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:47.300
appear different from their true form, in
this case—making someone appear as a wolf.&nbsp;

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:51.370
This werewolf glamour increased strength,
speed, and intelligence, all advantages to

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:54.110
help them kill and eat humans.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:58.830
Medieval Christian demonologists also believed
the Devil could appear in the form of a wolf.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:04.299
The rise in Christian doctrine condemning
witchcraft is only one factor as to why cannibalistic

00:17:04.299 --> 00:17:06.690
werewolf accusations rose.&nbsp;

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:08.581
The Black Death, or the bubonic plague, is
another.

00:17:08.581 --> 00:17:14.410
By the end of the 14th century, it is estimated
that&nbsp; anywhere from 20 to 75% of the global

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:17.330
population succumbed to the Black Death.&nbsp;

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:21.240
Fewer people meant less encroachment on the
wolves’ natural habitat, and therefore the

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:22.630
wolf population grew.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:27.199
In the 15th and 16th centuries, reports of
roving wolf packs increased.

00:17:27.199 --> 00:17:32.440
They ate livestock and occasionally attacked
humans, which corresponded to an increase

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.440
in werewolf accusations throughout Europe.&nbsp;

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:40.110
In France belief in werewolves, or in loup-garou
--men destined to become wolves at the full

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:45.120
moon,&nbsp; were at an all-time high during the
Inquisition, with 30,000 reported cases between

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.310
1520-1630, not surprising given the aim to
combat heresy.&nbsp;

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:51.470
And when French colonizers traveled to places
like Canada, Haiti, and Louisiana, the loup-garou

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:59.100
took on additional characteristics as it encountered
the folklore of the indigenous and enslaved

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:00.100
peoples.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:05.800
The myths and the ideas regarding werewolves
and wolves would travel to the new world with

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:08.650
the settlers and colonizers who came from
Europe.&nbsp;

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:13.850
These creatures that we had thought we had
destroyed or left behind in many ways, therefore

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:17.300
the landscape seemed to be a landscape of
nightmares.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:24.260
Now, the colonizers made use in some ways
of these preexisting myths, it gave a function

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:25.660
for why wolves had to be destroyed.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:33.800
Unfortunately, the hatred of wolves would
be misapplied as well to the indigenous population.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:41.120
And so the way that Walzer this seen as Savage
and voracious creatures was used in intimately

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:46.390
recessed and violent terms against the native
Americans themselves and the death of the

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:47.390
walls was done alongside the death of the
native Americans.&nbsp;

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:51.070
Werewolf trials in Central and Western Europe
peaked in the early-17th century.the label

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:56.140
“werewolf” could be applied to someone
who engaged in heinous crimes outside of witchcraft—like

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:01.340
murder, cannibalism, and rape
In many cases the shapeshifting was less significant

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:05.090
than the murder and cannibalism associated
with the change.&nbsp;

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:09.440
In fact, during the 16th century, many of
the werewolf executions reported by the Catholic

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:14.830
Church involved confessions of eating human
flesh, particularly that of children.

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:19.510
No 16th-century werewolf gained more notoriety
than German farmer Peeter Stubbe.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:23.510
It was said he had made a deal with the Devil
that allowed him to take the shape of a wolf

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:29.170
in order to kill men, women, and children
without getting caught—for over 25 years.

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:33.060
Stubbe was captured by a group of hunters
looking for the beast that had been slaughtering

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.050
their families and livestock.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:39.570
Their dogs succeeded in chasing down a wolf,
but the hunters lost sight of it—only to

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:41.720
see Stubbe in the same spot the wolf had vanished.&nbsp;

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:46.890
The hunters assumed he had removed his werewolf
girdle and regained his human form, After

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:51.800
a little torture on the rack, he quickly confessed
to procuring the girdle, becoming a wolf,

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:56.650
murdering 14 children and 2 pregnant women,
He reportedly killed his own son and ate his

00:19:56.650 --> 00:20:01.510
brain, committed incest with his daughter
and sister and was rumored to have a long

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:04.910
affair with a “she Devil” sent to him
by Satan.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:09.460
His daughter and mistress were also tried
and found guilty as accessories to the murders

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:13.880
and on October 31, 1589, they were all executed.&nbsp;

00:20:13.880 --> 00:20:19.040
Stubbe was laid on a wheel and burning pincers
were used to pull the flesh off his body.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:23.850
His legs and arms were broken, and his head
was removed, the body burned to ash.

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:27.100
His daughter and mistress were burned to death
with his corpse.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:32.030
Stubbe’s severed head was spiked on a pole
and displayed below a wooden carving of a

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.030
wolf.

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:37.570
The cruelty of the crimes and punishment were
immortalized in 1590 as an English narrative:

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:42.510
A True Discourse Declaring the Damnable Life
and Death of One Stubbe Peeter—the first

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:47.280
popular werewolf narrative in England that
reinvigorated Great Britain’s interest in

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:52.490
the monster after it had all but been eradicated
in the previous few centuries after aggressive

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:53.790
wolf cleansing campaigns.&nbsp;

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:59.130
The Stubbe case, and others like it, are today
recognized by many scholars as evidence of

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:03.560
early human serial killers rather than supernatural
werewolves.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:08.110
This reflects a movement that began hundreds
of years before as history attempted to rationalize

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:11.930
or explain the actions of those who claimed
to be wolf shapeshifters.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:17.440
While today there is a distinction between
the fictional werewolf and the condition of

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:21.250
lycanthropy, the two ideas were entwined for
most of history.&nbsp;

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:26.100
As early as the second century and well into
the 18th century, “Lycanthropy” could

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:30.070
refer to both the literal transformation of
human to wolf using magic&nbsp;

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:35.640
and to a form of mental illness where a person
believed they could become a wolf.&nbsp;

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:40.850
Today, physicians recognize ‘clinical lycanthropy’
or “lycomania” as a real disorder— a

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:44.930
rare condition where patients believe they
are transforming into or have transformed

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:47.100
into a non-human animal.

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:52.070
Another explanation behind werewolves is a
hallucination brought on by drugs.

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:55.750
Archeological and literary evidence suggests
that the ‘trance’ that endowed the berserkers

00:21:55.750 --> 00:22:00.750
with supposed increased strength and speed
may have been caused by the ingestion of hallucinogenic

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:02.110
mushrooms.&nbsp;

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:06.360
Another commonly cited hallucinogen that may
have contributed to werewolf delusions is

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.680
outbreaks of ergot poisoning.&nbsp;

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:13.640
The fungus can infect rye, wheat, barley,
and oats—common food sources—and when

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:18.420
ingested causes hallucinations and physical
symptoms like uncontrollable body movements,

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:21.060
panic attacks, a burning sensation, and gangrene.&nbsp;

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:32.320
As recently as in 1951, victims of ergot poisoning
in France reported werewolf hallucinations.

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:40.760
Yet another proposed medical explanation for
werewolf lore is&nbsp; . Also called “werewolf

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:42.490
syndrome” or “Ambras syndrome.”

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:46.700
The disease causes extreme hair growth that
often covers the entire body.&nbsp;

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:51.190
Documented since the 17th century, it is a
recognized condition today believed to be

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:52.700
caused by gene mutation.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:57.750
An excess of hair in unusual places was often
cited as evidence of a werewolf in their human

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:01.990
form, so some accused werewolves may have
just been suffering from this condition.

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:07.740
Then in the 20th century, two new explanations
appeared: porphyria and rabies.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:13.060
Porphyria is a rare genetic disease characterized
by severe photosensitivity that results in

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:19.660
some stereotypical werewolf traits: hyperpigmentation,
excessive hair growth, nocturnal wandering,

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:21.050
and red or reddish-brown teeth.&nbsp;

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:26.330
The rabies virus causes crazed and frenzied
behavior in animals (including wolves) who

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:30.890
are driven to more aggressive behavior when
the virus attacks the brain, often against

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:33.360
other animals and even humans.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:39.280
In humans, symptoms include difficulty swallowing,
increased thirst, inflammation of the brain,

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:44.340
hypersexuality, irrational fear of water,
contraction of the lips—and although extremely

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:47.020
rare, the desire to bite other humans.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:52.690
I can't count the number of times that people
have explained to me that the reason why anyone

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:59.800
has ever believed in werewolves is because
of porphyria or ergot poisoning or hyper hirsutism.

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:06.230
And I find this a really flawed way of thinking
about where wolves and monsters more generally.

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:12.300
I think tried to explain the way beliefs actually
destroys the power and the wonder of the supernatural

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:15.850
and also the symbolism and importance of monsters.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:21.480
I think really it's far better to allow werewolves,
to remain a creature that can be interpreted

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.760
in a multiplicity of different ways.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:30.360
And for us to understand that where wolves,
wolves, and other monsters remain powerful

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:32.990
symbols within the human imagination.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:37.350
The proposed explanations for werewolves aren’t
quite as numerous as the different variations

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:42.780
of this monster, but they indicate a desire
to understand&nbsp; strange and deviant behavior.&nbsp;

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:46.750
Given that wolves were at one point the most
common and dangerous land-based predators

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:51.500
in Europe and did pose an immediate threat
to humans, their connection to witchcraft

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:55.870
and werewolves—other perceived threats to
humanity—isn’t all that surprising.

00:24:55.870 --> 00:25:00.559
Fear of the werewolf in the Medieval and Early
Modern period seems to be about the possibility

00:25:00.559 --> 00:25:02.750
that the body is malleable.

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:07.300
The mental capacity to shed one’s identity
so dramatically, into another “body” or

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:12.110
persona that seems more monstrous, violent,
and unpredictable, may have helped us explain

00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:14.940
the crimes humanity can commit against one
another.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:19.770
The werewolf is one of the most well-known
monsters we’ve covered on Monstrum, and

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:22.080
one that has endured for centuries.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:23.670
But why?

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:27.360
In part two of the series, we’ll cover how
the Werewolf has continued to claw at our

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:42.750
modern imagainations.

